/target
**/target

# The fixture workspaces under tests/ are built by the integration tests, each into
# its own scratch target dir. None of that is source.
/tests/fixtures/**/target
/tests/scratch

# The repository root ignores Cargo.lock (right for a tree of libraries). This
# module's lock is re-included, because it is load-bearing:
#
#   * CI runs every build with `--locked`, which is a no-op assertion without a
#     committed lock and an outright failure with one missing;
#   * a security tool that resolved its own dependencies at build time is not the
#     artefact anyone reviewed — and this module's whole subject is what a build
#     resolves and then executes. Floating our own dependency versions while
#     selling a build-time capability policy would be the joke telling itself.
!Cargo.lock

# The fixtures' own locks are not. They are separate workspaces with no registry
# dependencies at all, generated the first time a test runs cargo on them, and
# nothing builds them with `--locked`. Committing them would make an integration
# run dirty the working tree, which turns every `git status` after a test into a
# question somebody has to answer.
/tests/fixtures/**/Cargo.lock

# Never commit a signing key. Licence and attestation keys are generated on an
# offline host and must not enter any repository; this is the belt to that braces.
#
# `*.pkcs8` is the one that actually fires. It is the DEFAULT filename the
# key-generating commands write and read, so it is the name that ends up in a
# working tree — and it was the one extension missing here. The publish
# allowlist already excludes it and the CI invariants job already scans
# for it; git was the gap between them.
*.seed
*.pem
*.key
*.pkcs8

# The repository root ignores `*.txt` wholesale. The benign fixture reads a table
# from its manifest directory precisely because that is what a real code generator
# does, so the input file is source, not output. Without this negation the fixture
# silently falls back to a built-in table and stops testing the read path at all —
# which is the kind of test that passes while measuring nothing.
!tests/fixtures/**/inputs/*.txt

# Same blanket `*.txt` rule, same failure mode, one directory over. `API.txt` is the
# committed snapshot of the sandbox crate's public surface, and `crates/sandbox/tests/api.rs`
# reads it to catch a `pub` item added without a decision. Untracked, it is present in a
# working tree and absent in a fresh CI checkout — so the gate passes for the author and
# panics for everyone else, which is worse than not having it. The whole point of the file
# is that enlarging the promise is a line in a diff; that only works if the file is in git.
!crates/sandbox/API.txt

# The fuzz crate's build output and a fuzzer's own scratch. `fuzz/corpus` is NOT here and
# must not be: the seeds are committed, `cargo test` reads them, and a crash committed
# there is what turns a fuzzer finding into a permanent regression test on the stable
# toolchain. `cargo fuzz init` generates a .gitignore that hides the corpus; that is the
# opposite of what this project wants from one.
/fuzz/artifacts
/fuzz/coverage
